window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"][6]={"university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M6-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M6-L1 – Measurement Chains, Sensors and Measurement Points<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Bachelor: Measurement and Introduction to Control<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Resistors and\nvoltage dividers<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Thévenin\nequivalent circuit<\/a>, units and orders of magnitude from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Describe a measurement chain\nfrom the sensor to the estimated value, calculate the loading effect of\na finite input resistance and assess the resolution of idealised\ndigitisation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From the measurement location to a numerical value<\/h2>\n<p>The <strong>measurand<\/strong> is the quantity you want to determine.\nFor the temperature of a body, for example, it must be clear at which\nlocation and for which time interval the value is to apply. A sensor\nresponds to a local physical quantity and converts it into, for example,\na resistance or a voltage. Signal conditioning adapts the signal range\nand can attenuate unwanted frequency components. The analogue-to-digital\nconverter (ADC) supplies a code sampled in time. Processing converts\nthat code back into the unit of the measurand using a model.<\/p>\n<p>Label each interface in a block diagram with its signal and unit.\nThis helps to isolate the source of a discrepancy. An unexpected\ntemperature reading may arise from the measurement location, loading at\nthe sensor interface, limited ADC resolution or the conversion back to\nthe measurand. A stable reading only demonstrates that the displayed\nnumbers vary little.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Calculating input loading<\/h2>\n<p>Replace the sensor output circuit at the measurement point with its\nopen-circuit voltage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand series resistance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\mathrm{th}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the DC model, the measuring instrument has input resistance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\mathrm{in}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mfrac><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_\\mathrm{in}=U_0\\frac{R_\\mathrm{in}}{R_\\mathrm{th}+R_\\mathrm{in}},\\qquad\n\\frac{U_\\mathrm{in}-U_0}{U_0}=-\\frac{R_\\mathrm{th}}{R_\\mathrm{th}+R_\\mathrm{in}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The second expression applies for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_0\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe magnitude of the relative error decreases as the ratio\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\mathrm{in}/R_\\mathrm{th}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincreases. For it to be at most one per cent,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>99<\/mn><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\mathrm{in}\\ge99R_\\mathrm{th}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust hold.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_016_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Measurement chain from temperature through sensor resistance, loaded input voltage and amplification to ADC code. The input resistance draws current and reduces the input voltage.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The measurement chain converts temperature through\nsensor resistance, loaded sensor voltage and amplification into an ADC\ncode; input resistance affects the signal.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model calculation from the measurement point to the ADC<\/h2>\n<p>In the model, a sensor with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nforms the lower resistor in a voltage divider. The upper resistor is\nalso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the ideal supply is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.3\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe open-circuit voltage is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.65<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.65\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the output resistance of the parallel combination is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\mathrm{in}=100\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyou obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.57143<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_\\mathrm{in}=1.57143\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLoading therefore causes an error of about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>78.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-78.6\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4.76<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-4.76\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrelative to the open-circuit value. The direct model with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>∥<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.0909<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\mathrm{Sensor}\\parallel R_\\mathrm{in}=9.0909\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalso gives this result.<\/p>\n<p>An idealised amplifier stage with gain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsupplies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.14286<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.14286\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLet the subsequent\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>12<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-bit\nADC divide the range from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.096<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4.096\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>12<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4096<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^{12}=4096<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nequally wide intervals. The step size is then<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4.096<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>4096<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=\\frac{4.096\\,\\mathrm V}{4096}=1\\,\\mathrm{mV}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With coding based on the lower interval boundary as the transition,\nthe code is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>3142<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3142<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nReconstruction using the interval midpoint gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.1425<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.1425\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe difference of around\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.36<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.36\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis within half a step. An additional ADC bit would reduce the\nquantisation step. The input loading that occurred before amplification\nwould still be present.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Compare\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\mathrm{in}=1\\,\\mathrm{M\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the same divider.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Determine the step size of an ideal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>10<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-bit\nADC with the same input voltage range. State the maximum quantisation\nerror when reconstructing at the interval midpoint within the\nmeasurement range.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Solution 1.<\/strong> For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{M\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe loading factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1000<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1005<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1000/1005<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrelative to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.65<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.65\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives an input voltage of around\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.6418<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.6418\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe magnitude of the error drops from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>78.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">78.6\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8.2\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution 2.<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.096<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1024<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4.096\\,\\mathrm V/1024=4\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the chosen model the magnitude of the error is at most\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn input outside the permissible range causes saturation and falls\noutside this estimate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations and further learning<\/h2>\n<p>For time-dependent signals, an input can also have a capacitive\neffect. The loading effect then depends on the frequency and on the time\nbetween two samples. The ADC calculation here assumes ideal, equally\nwide intervals. Deriving temperature from a resistance or code\nadditionally requires a calibration relationship. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L2<\/a>\nshows how to deal with correction and uncertainty.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/10-4-electrical-measuring-instruments\">OpenStax,\nUniversity Physics Volume 2, § 10.4 – Electrical Measuring\nInstruments<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 10.4 – internal resistances and instrument\nloading. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-003-signals-and-systems-fall-2011/resources/lecture-22-sampling-and-quantization/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.003 – Sampling and Quantization<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · sampling and\nquantisation. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M6-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M6-L2 – Calibration, Error and Measurement Uncertainty<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Bachelor: Measurement and Introduction to Control<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Measurement\nchains and resolution<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">statistical\nmodelling<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Determine a linear calibration\nrelationship from two reference points, correct a series of measurements\nand combine independent uncertainty contributions into a standard\nuncertainty.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Calibration and dispersion<\/h2>\n<p>A temperature sensor displays similar values in repeated\nmeasurements. A comparison with a reference under specified conditions\nreveals whether these values estimate the temperature well.\n<strong>Calibration<\/strong> establishes the relationship between the\nreference values and the corresponding indications, together with their\nrespective uncertainties. Correction subsequently uses this relationship\nto estimate the measurand. Changing the instrument settings would\nconstitute adjustment.<\/p>\n<p>A systematic error may recur in a similar way under unchanged\nconditions. Random error varies between repetitions. The mean of\nrepeated observations can reduce some of the random dispersion. A shift\nshared by all indications remains in the mean.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Inverting a calibration function<\/h2>\n<p>The example values denote temperature deviations in kelvin. Let the\nreference value be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the indication\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWithin the operating range we use the model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=a+bx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTwo reference pairs give<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Reference\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin K<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Indication\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin K<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">10.00<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">10.30<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">30.00<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">30.50<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>From this follow\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>30.50<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>10.30<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>30.00<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>10.00<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.01<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=(30.50-10.30)/(30.00-10.00)=1.01<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=0.20\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe corrected estimate for a new indication is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat x=(y-a)/b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>Five repeated readings are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>20.2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>20.3<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>20.4<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>20.3<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20.3\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe mean is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20.30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar y=20.30\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>19.901<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat x\\approx19.901\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe correction lies between the reference points. A third reference\nmeasurement in the same range would be useful for checking the assumed\nlinearity. The two reference points themselves already define a unique\nstraight line.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Uncertainty budget for the mean<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependent repetitions under stable conditions, we use the sample\nstandard deviation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the corresponding standard uncertainty of the mean:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/munderover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><msqrt><mi>n<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_y=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(y_i-\\bar y)^2}{n-1}},\\qquad\nu(\\bar y)=\\frac{s_y}{\\sqrt n}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In the measurement series, the sum of squared deviations is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.02<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.02\\,\\mathrm{K^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.07071<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_y\\approx0.07071\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe contribution from repeated observations to the corrected temperature\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.03131<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u(\\bar y)/|b|\\approx0.03131\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor this teaching example, let a separate calibration characterisation\nsupply further standard uncertainties of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.060<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.060\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the transferred reference and calibration influence and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.030<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.030\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor an independent stability influence. Both are already expressed on\nthe corrected temperature scale. Each influence occurs only once in the\nbudget.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Influence<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Standard uncertainty in K<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Repeatability of the mean, corrected scale<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.03131<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reference and calibration in the teaching model<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.060<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Separate stability influence<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.030<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>For independent contributions, the variances add. This gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>0.03131<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mn>0.060<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mn>0.030<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.074<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_c=\\sqrt{0.03131^2+0.060^2+0.030^2}\\,\\mathrm K\\approx0.074\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nYou can report the result as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>19.901<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat x=19.901\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith a combined standard uncertainty of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.074<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.074\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA coverage probability cannot be derived from this statement alone.\nClassification as Type A or Type B describes how a contribution is\nevaluated, for example from statistical repetition data or reference\ninformation.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_132_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Calibration line through two reference pairs, illustrating inversion of the mean, with bars for the three standard uncertainty contributions.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The calibration line converts an indication into a\ncorrected reference value; repeatability, calibration and stability\nenter as separate uncertainty contributions.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The orange guide lines show how the corrected reference value is\nestimated from the indication. The three bars share a common scale and\nshow standard uncertainties, not variances. Their lengths are therefore\nnot added linearly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the corrected estimate for an indication of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>25.45<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">25.45\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to the repeatability contribution and the calibration\ncontribution if there are 20 independent repetitions instead of five,\nwith the same dispersion?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Solution 1.<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>25.45<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1.01<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>25.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(25.45-0.20)/1.01=25.00\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution 2.<\/strong> The repeatability contribution halves\nbecause the denominator contains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mi>n<\/mi><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe calibration influence shared by all repetitions remains. Under the\nsame remaining assumptions,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndrops to around\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.069<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.069\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations and further learning<\/h2>\n<p>In a practical uncertainty budget, parameters such as offset and\nslope may be correlated. Their covariances must then be included in the\ncalculation. Temperature drift over the measurement series may also\nviolate the independence assumption. The static model describes the\nrelationship after settling. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L3<\/a>\nadds the temporal response.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.bipm.org/en/doi/10.59161/jcgm200-2012\">BIPM/JCGM\n200:2012, International Vocabulary of Metrology<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · terms 2.16–2.19,\n2.26 and 2.39. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.bipm.org/en/doi/10.59161/jcgm100-2008e\">BIPM/JCGM\n100:2008, Guide to the Expression of Uncertainty in Measurement<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nsections 4 and 5, evaluation and combination of standard uncertainties.\n· Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M6-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M6-L3 – Models and Identification from a Step Response<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Bachelor: Measurement and Introduction to Control<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Differential\nequations for models<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">circuit step\nresponse<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">calibration<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Determine the static gain, time\nconstant and any detectable dead time from an idealised step response,\nand distinguish their effects.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why the plant needs time<\/h2>\n<p>The input to a model can change abruptly while its output follows\nwith a delay. A plant with a slow response smooths rapid input changes.\nDead time shifts the response additionally, for example due to transport\nor the temporal assignment of measurement data. As an introduction, we\nconsider a linear first-order plant with delay. The experiment begins in\nsteady state.<\/p>\n<p>With the change in the manipulated variable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ninitial value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nprocess gain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ntime constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand delay\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe ideal response to an input step at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mi>t<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(t)=\n\\begin{cases}\ny_0,&amp;0\\le t&lt;L,\\\\\ny_0+K\\Delta u\\bigl(1-\\exp(-(t-L)/\\tau)\\bigr),&amp;t\\ge L.\n\\end{cases}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The final value is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_\\infty=y_0+K\\Delta u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas the unit of the output quantity divided by the unit of the input\nquantity.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare expressed in units of time.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_017_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Simulated curve with 10 seconds of dead time. At 50 seconds the temperature reaches just under 3.26 kelvin, corresponding to 63.2 per cent of the change from 2 to 4 kelvin.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: After a 10 s dead time, the modelled temperature\ndeviation rises from 2 K towards 4 K; at 50 s it has reached 63.2% of\nthe total change.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reading parameters from the data<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Determine the initial value and a stable final value from the time\nintervals before and long after the step. The estimate is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=(y_\\infty-y_0)/\\Delta u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Estimate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom the time between the input change and the start of the visible\noutput change. With noisy data, this step requires a justified\nrule.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Determine the target value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>63<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_{63}=y_0+(1-\\exp(-1))(y_\\infty-y_0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nObtain the corresponding crossing time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>63<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_{63}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom the measured points or suitable interpolation. Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>63<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=t_{63}-L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>The figure of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>63.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">63.2\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrefers to the <strong>change<\/strong>. It also applies to a falling\noutput, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_\\infty-y_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nretaining its negative sign. The relationship follows from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=L+\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsince the remaining exponential term is then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exp(-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evaluating a simulated step in the manipulated variable<\/h2>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na dimensionless manipulated variable jumps from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.25<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.50<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe previously stable output is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.00\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the simulated long-term plateau is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4.00\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA densely sampled curve reveals a delay of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSome sample values from the simulation are:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Time in s<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Temperature deviation in K<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2.0000<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">10<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2.0000<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">30<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2.7869<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">50<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3.2642<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">90<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3.7293<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">170<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3.9634<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>From\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta u=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the output change of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfollows\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=8\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper unit of the manipulated variable. The target value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>63<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_{63}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis around\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.2642<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.2642\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe curve reaches this at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=40\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCheck the estimate at a second time. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>90<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">90\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ntwo time constants have elapsed since the start of the output response.\nThe model gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.7293<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2+2(1-\\exp(-2))\\approx3.7293\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For the same model, calculate the time at which the output reaches\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.00\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Another plant starts at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand, after an input step of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nreaches a final value of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe response starts after\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand reaches\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>9.3679<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9.3679\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">25\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhat parameters do you estimate?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Solution 1.<\/strong> The remaining fraction of the initial\ndifference is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTherefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>37.7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=10\\,\\mathrm s+40\\,\\mathrm s\\ln 2\\approx37.7\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution 2.<\/strong> The quotient of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=5\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper unit of the manipulated variable. The dead time is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the time constant is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">25\\,\\mathrm s-5\\,\\mathrm s=20\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations and further learning<\/h2>\n<p>An unknown final value and a dead time resolved only coarsely make\nthe graphical estimate sensitive. A slowly starting higher-order\nresponse can resemble dead time. Therefore, check the fitted model\nagainst additional data points or a second step in the manipulated\nvariable. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L4<\/a>\nconnects the measured and manipulated variables in a feedback loop.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://apmonitor.com/pdc/index.php/Main/FirstOrderSystems\">APMonitor\n– First Order Plus Dead Time<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · FOPDT model equation and parameters:\nprocess gain, time constant and dead time; original teaching examples. ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ctms.engin.umich.edu/CTMS/index.php?example=Introduction&amp;section=ControlPID&amp;section=SystemAnalysis\">University\nof Michigan – Control Tutorials, Introduction: System Analysis<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nFirst-order systems: time constant, pole and step response; comparison\nwith the teaching model. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M6-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M6-L4 – Open- and Closed-Loop Control<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Bachelor: Measurement and Introduction to Control<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Signals and\nsystems<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Laplace\ntransform<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">plant model\nfrom the step response<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Identify the reference, control\nerror, manipulated variable, disturbance and measured value in a block\ndiagram, and calculate the effect of simple negative feedback.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Open-loop and closed-loop control of the same process<\/h2>\n<p>A modelled heating process is intended to achieve a constant\ntemperature deviation. In <strong>open-loop control<\/strong>, a command\ndetermines the manipulated variable. In <strong>closed-loop\ncontrol<\/strong>, the measured output feeds back into the manipulated\nvariable. If the temperature falls below the reference, suitable\nnegative feedback increases the manipulated variable.<\/p>\n<p>The model distinguishes five quantities. The reference\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndetermines the desired value. The output\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the temperature deviation to be controlled. The measuring element\nprovides\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhich equals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the simplest model. The error\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>e<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e=r-y_m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis passed to the controller. Its manipulated variable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nacts on the plant. A disturbance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrepresents an additional influence, such as a load change. The point at\nwhich the disturbance enters matters for every calculation.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_015_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Control loop with a negative measurement feedback path. The reference and the returned measurement are compared, the controller converts the error into a manipulated variable, and a disturbance is added at the output.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: In the closed loop, the measured feedback is\nsubtracted from the reference; the controller and plant affect the\noutput, where a disturbance is added.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Translating feedback into equations<\/h2>\n<p>We use linear transfer elements for the controller\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand plant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G(s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith zero initial conditions. The disturbance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis added at the plant output. In this model the measured value equals\nthe disturbed output. Capital letters denote Laplace transforms.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=C(R-Y),\\qquad Y=GU+D.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Substituting and rearranging gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>R<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>D<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=\\frac{GC}{1+GC}R+\\frac{1}{1+GC}D.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The first factor describes the reference path, the second the\ndisturbance path. The loop gain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">GC<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndetermines the frequencies at which these factors are large or small.\nFor an additive disturbance at the plant input, however, the disturbance\npath becomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G/(1+GC)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe location in the block diagram therefore determines the numerator.\nThe algebraic formula also provides the characteristic condition for the\ndynamics. Whether the feedback is stable is examined in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example with a load change<\/h2>\n<p>For the static comparison, let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=Ku+d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=2\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper dimensionless unit of the manipulated variable and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=2\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFeedforward sets\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_0=r/K=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWithout feedback,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=u_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains unchanged. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=-0.4\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\noccurs, the output consequently drops to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.6\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>Add proportional feedback\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=u_0+k_p(r-y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_p=0.5\\,\\mathrm K^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mi>d<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=r+\\frac{d}{1+Kk_p}=2\\,\\mathrm K-\\frac{0.4\\,\\mathrm K}{2}=1.8\\,\\mathrm K.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The static disturbance effect is halved. The control error of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.2\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nproduces an additional manipulated-variable contribution of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=1.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe plant again gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\cdot1.1\\,\\mathrm K-0.4\\,\\mathrm K=1.8\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe dimensionless manipulated variable is unbounded in this algebraic\nexercise.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What temperature deviation does the model give with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_p=1\\,\\mathrm K^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the same disturbance?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The sensor adds an error\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOtherwise under the same assumptions, derive the transfer factor from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto the actual output\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Solution 1.<\/strong> The DC loop gain is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Kk_p=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.867<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=2\\,\\mathrm K-0.4\\,\\mathrm K/3\\approx1.867\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe residual error is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.133<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.133\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution 2.<\/strong> From\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=GC(R-Y-N)+D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfollows the factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-GC/(1+GC)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA positive measurement offset can reduce the actual output because the\ncontroller interprets the measured value as too high.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limitations and further study<\/h2>\n<p>The static example neglects energy storage, dead times and actuator\nlimits. In time-dependent plants, increased gain may improve disturbance\nrejection while reducing the stability margin. Measurement errors and\noutput disturbances act through different paths. In <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L5<\/a>,\nyou compare the dynamics of P, PI and PID controllers.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/16-30-feedback-control-systems-fall-2010/resources/mit16_30f10_lec24/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 16.30 Feedback Control Systems, Lecture 24 – Closed-Loop\nSystem Analysis<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · feedback and closed-loop analysis. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cds.caltech.edu/~murray/books/AM05/pdf/am08-pid_19Jul11.pdf\">Åström/Murray\n– Feedback Systems v2.10d, Chapter 10 PID Control<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 10.1,\npp. 294–297 and § 10.5, p. 308: proportional, integral and derivative\naction and filtered derivative. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M6-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M6-L5 – Stability and Introduction to Controllers<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Bachelor: Measurement and Introduction to Control<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Laplace\ntransform and poles<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">first-order\nplant<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">negative\nfeedback<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Determine the poles of a simple\ncontinuous-time control loop and compare the steady-state error,\ntransient response and noise sensitivity of P, PI and PID\ncontrollers.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Poles and controller types<\/h2>\n<p>Let a first-order lag plant be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G(s)=K/(\\tau s+1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe consider continuous-time linear models with negative unity feedback.\nA pole\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith a negative real part produces a decaying component\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exp(pt)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor asymptotic internal stability, all dynamically relevant eigenvalues\nmust lie in the open left half-plane. A transfer function after\ncancellation may conceal an internal mode, so we consider the\ncharacteristic polynomial before cancellation.<\/p>\n<p>In the parallel form, a PID controller is written as<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>s<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(s)=k_p+\\frac{k_i}{s}+k_d s.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The P term responds to the current error. The I term accumulates the\nerror. The D term accounts for its rate of change. For a fixed control\nobjective, the D contribution vanishes when the error is constant. With\na dimensionless manipulated variable and a temperature error in kelvin,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhave the units\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm K^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\mathrm{K\\,s})^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{s\\,K^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>P and PI on the same plant<\/h2>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=k_p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe reference-to-output transfer function is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{Y}{R}=\\frac{Kk_p}{\\tau s+1+Kk_p}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The pole lies at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=-(1+Kk_p)/\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a stable loop and a constant reference\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe remaining error is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_0/(1+Kk_p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA PI controller now adds\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_i/s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe denominator of the reference-to-output transfer function before\ncancellation is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau s^2+(1+Kk_p)s+Kk_i .<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For the real coefficients of this second-order polynomial, positive\ncoefficients are sufficient for stability. With a positive stability\nmargin, an attainable reference and an unlimited ideal integrator,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>e<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust hold in steady state: a persistently non-zero error would continue\nto change the integrator contribution to the manipulated variable. This\nargument assumes that the state under consideration has actually reached\nsteady state.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Numerical example with the same P term<\/h2>\n<p>Set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=2\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper unit of the manipulated variable,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=4\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_p=0.5\\,\\mathrm K^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the P controller, the pole lies at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.5\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA reference step of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nleads to a steady-state temperature deviation of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a steady-state error of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>For the PI controller, choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.125<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_i=0.125\\,(\\mathrm{K\\,s})^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe numerical coefficients of the denominator give\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>s<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4s^2+2s+0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis expressed in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis gives a double pole at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.25\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe steady-state output equals the reference. This choice leads to\npole-zero cancellation at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.25\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the ideal reference path. Both poles before cancellation are already\nstable.<\/p>\n<p>Adding the ideal D term\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_d=0.25\\,\\mathrm{s\\,K^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchanges the leading coefficient to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau+Kk_d=4.5\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.5<\/mn><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>s<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4.5s^2+2s+0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.2222<\/mn><mo>±<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>0.0786<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p\\approx(-0.2222\\pm\\mathrm j\\,0.0786)\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe poles remain stable. This change does not imply a general\nimprovement for other plants.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_133_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Analytical reference responses to a step of two kelvin: P approaches one kelvin, PI approaches two kelvin.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: For the same 2 K setpoint step, the modelled P\ncontroller approaches only 1 K, whereas the PI controller approaches 2\nK; these responses exclude a load disturbance.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>In the ideal P model, the response to the reference step is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)=1\\,\\mathrm K\\,(1-\\mathrm e^{-t/(2\\,\\mathrm s)})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the PI controller chosen here, the stable cancellation in the\nreference path gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)=2\\,\\mathrm K\\,(1-\\mathrm e^{-t/(4\\,\\mathrm s)})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure assumes a zero initial value and includes no actuator\nlimits.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which proportional gain gives a pole at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the same plant? What is the resulting steady-state error for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_0=2\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How can you tell from an ideal P or PI model whether the output\nreaches a constant reference without steady-state error in the stable\ncase?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Solution 1.<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-(1+2k_p)/4=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_p=1.5\\,\\mathrm K^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe error is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm K/(1+2\\cdot1.5)=0.5\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution 2.<\/strong> The reference-to-output transfer\nfunction at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust equal one. P gives the factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Kk_p/(1+Kk_p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor finite\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nPI with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_i&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas a factor of one in the model described, because the numerator and\ndenominator have the same constant term\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Kk_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limitations and further study<\/h2>\n<p>Ideal differentiation amplifies rapidly varying disturbance\ncomponents. Practical designs often use a filtered D term and\ndifferentiate the measured value to avoid demanding an ideal impulse in\nresponse to a reference step. Actuator limits can cause integrator\nwindup and subsequently worsen the transient response. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L6<\/a>\nexamines P and PI with a modelled actuator limit and a load\ndisturbance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cds.caltech.edu/~murray/books/AM05/pdf/am08-pid_19Jul11.pdf\">Åström/Murray\n– Feedback Systems v2.10d, Chapter 10 PID Control<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 10.1,\npp. 294–297 and § 10.5, p. 308: proportional, integral and derivative\naction and filtered derivative. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/16-30-feedback-control-systems-fall-2010/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 16.30 Feedback Control Systems – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nLectures 2, 23 and 24 on poles, integrator limiting and closed loops. ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M6-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M6-L6 – Temperature Control Case Study<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Bachelor: Measurement and Introduction to Control<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Plant\nmodel<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">feedback<\/a>\nand <a href=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">P and\nPI controllers<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Reproduce a simulated\ntemperature control system, compare reference and disturbance responses\nusing defined metrics, and document the range of validity of the\nresults.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A testable temperature model<\/h2>\n<p>The state\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the temperature deviation relative to an environment assumed to be\nconstant. For the dimensionless manipulated variable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a disturbance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin kelvin, we use<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">60\\,\\mathrm s\\,\\dot x+x=(10\\,\\mathrm K)u+d,\\qquad 0\\le u\\le1.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstarts at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the new reference\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>240<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=240\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchanges from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe modelled sensor supplies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndirectly. Both variants use\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_p=0.20\\,\\mathrm K^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe PI controller adds\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_i=k_p/(60\\,\\mathrm s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts integrator contribution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstarts at zero. The unconstrained manipulated variable is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_\\mathrm{raw}=k_p(r-x)+z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand is limited to the range from zero to one.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Simulation with standard Python<\/h2>\n<p>The script updates the state and integrator using the explicit Euler\nmethod. Both calculations use the error at the beginning of the step. If\nthe manipulated variable reaches a limit, the integrator may continue\nonly when its contribution acts towards the usable range.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> simulate(ki, dt<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">0.25<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    x <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> z <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    rows <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> []<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> k <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">480<\/span> <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> dt) <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        t <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> k <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> dt<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        d <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.0<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> t <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">240<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        e <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">4.0<\/span> <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> x<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        raw <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.20<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> e <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> z<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        u <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">min<\/span>(<span class=\"fl\">1.0<\/span>, <span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"fl\">0.0<\/span>, raw))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        rows.append((t, x, u))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        x <span class=\"op\">+=<\/span> dt <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> (<span class=\"fl\">10.0<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> u <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> d <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> x) <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">60.0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        within <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.0<\/span> <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> raw <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1.0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        returns <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (raw <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.0<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> e <span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.0<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> (raw <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1.0<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> e <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.0<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> within <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> returns:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            z <span class=\"op\">+=<\/span> dt <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> ki <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> e<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> rows<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> name, ki, plateau <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> ((<span class=\"st\">&quot;P&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.0<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">8.0<\/span> <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>), (<span class=\"st\">&quot;PI&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.20<\/span> <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">60<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">4.0<\/span>)):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    rows <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> simulate(ki)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    rise <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"bu\">next<\/span>(t <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> t, x, _ <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> rows <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> t <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">240<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> x <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> f <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> plateau)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> f <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"fl\">0.10<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.90<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    pre <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [x <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> t, x, _ <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> rows <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> t <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">240<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(name, <span class=\"st\">&quot;Rise time&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(rise[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>] <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> rise[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>], <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>), <span class=\"st\">&quot;s&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;Overshoot&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"fl\">0.0<\/span>, <span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(pre) <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> plateau), <span class=\"st\">&quot;K&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> t, x, _ <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> rows:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> t <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"fl\">240.0<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">280.0<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">480.0<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"ss\">f&quot;t=<\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>t<span class=\"sc\">:.0f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> s: x=<\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>x<span class=\"sc\">:.4f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> K&quot;<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Metrics and plausibility check<\/h2>\n<p>Here the rise time measures the interval between\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>90<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">90\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof the final steady-state value without a disturbance for each\ncontroller. Under this definition a P controller may be fast and still\nmiss the actual reference. Overshoot is measured relative to the same\nsteady-state comparison value in the time window before the\ndisturbance.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Quantity<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">P<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">PI<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Rise time in s<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">43.75<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">65.75<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Overshoot before disturbance in K<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>240<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(240\\,\\mathrm s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin K<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2.6667<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3.9987<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>280<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(280\\,\\mathrm s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin K<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2.3779<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3.7491<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>480<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(480\\,\\mathrm s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin K<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2.3333<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3.9822<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The steady-state P reference follows from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=10k_p(4-x)+d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=(8+d)/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen all temperatures are expressed numerically in kelvin. Before and\nafter the disturbance, the expected values are therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.6667<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.6667\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.3333<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.3333\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe PI controller can eventually return to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe required manipulated variable increases from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.4<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.5<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth values lie within the permitted range. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>480<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">480\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe disturbance response is still decaying. In both evaluated\ntrajectories, the manipulated variable remains between zero and one\nwithout reaching the limits.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_134_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Simulated temperature trajectories for P and PI from the start to 480 seconds, with a reference of four kelvin and a marked load disturbance at 240 seconds.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: After the modelled load step at 240 s, P retains a\nsteady-state offset while PI returns close to the 4 K setpoint. These\nare simulation curves.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The figure uses the state values calculated by the script. The P\ncontroller retains a steady-state error both before and after the load\nstep. The PI controller approaches the reference again after the load\nstep. The values are numerical model results, not measured curves.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and solution outlines<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Simulate with half the step size. Compare the values at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>280<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">280\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand document the difference.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Under what conditions would a specified reference be unattainable\neven with integral action if the disturbance remained at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=-1\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Solution 1.<\/strong> Set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.125<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dt=0.125<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the function call. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>280<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">280\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe results remain close to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.3779<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.3779\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.7491<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.7491\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nrespectively. The difference due to halving the step size is less than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.001<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.001\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin both cases. The input and output time points must coincide for the\ncomparison.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution 2.<\/strong> With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u\\in[0,1]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nonly the steady-state range from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be reached. A reference above\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor below\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains outside the range of possible steady-state values.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations and next steps<\/h2>\n<p>This case study examines an idealised model with a constant\nenvironment and a single time constant. Delay, measurement noise,\ncalibration errors and additional thermal storage can change the\nresults. A follow-up project can add each influence separately and check\nit against the reference documented here. The <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;page=study-path&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">study\npathway<\/a> leads to subsequent modules.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/16-30-feedback-control-systems-fall-2010/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 16.30 Feedback Control Systems – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nLectures 23 and 24 on integrator limiting and closed loops. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cds.caltech.edu/~murray/books/AM05/pdf/am08-pid_19Jul11.pdf\">Åström/Murray\n– Feedback Systems v2.10d, Chapter 10 PID Control<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 10.1,\npp. 294–297 and § 10.5, p. 308: proportional, integral and derivative\naction and filtered derivative. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://apmonitor.com/pdc/index.php/Main/FirstOrderSystems\">APMonitor\n– First Order Plus Dead Time<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · FOPDT model equation and parameters:\nprocess gain, time constant and dead time; original teaching examples. ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
